## 2. Formation of 2D nanosheets and their composite membranes
## 2.1. Graphene-based materials
Graphene-based materials, such as porous graphene, GO, and rGO, are one of the first developed examples of 2D membranes. From the porous graphene membranes prepared by oxidative etching or nitrogen functionalization to the latest rGO membranes by post-reduction of GO after forming a membrane layer, graphene-based membranes have exhibited a potential for gas, water, and ion separation applications. Graphene-based membranes have been introduced to other polymer-based membranes to give enhanced properties such as processability and anti-fouling capabilities. Most of the membranes are prepared on a porous substrate as a thin film composite. Facile preparation methods such as vacuum filtration and spin-coating for membrane fabrication with large areas also brought the possibility of graphene-based membranes to industrial applications. However, for gas separation and water treatment applications, which are the most widely applied membrane processes, graphene-based membranes are currently inappropriate because of their low separation efficiency and performance instability for long-term membrane operations. To overcome these drawbacks, modification of GO by crosslinking has been widely studied. Hydroxyl and carboxyl groups on GO are the main groups that are used to functionalize GO because of their high reactivity. The amount of hydroxyl and carboxyl groups on GO can be controlled during GO preparation.
A covalent crosslinking by esterification or amidation via a coupling reaction with small molecules or polymers is a facile modification method that controls the stability of GO nanosheets and the molecular transport properties of GO membranes.[8] Crosslinked GO was developed as a layered material to improve its mechanical properties such as strength, stiffness, and toughness. GO-based layered composite materials including GOpoly(vinyl alcohol),[9] GO-poly(methyl methacrylate),[10] GOborate,[11] and GO-polydopamine,[12] were fabricated by crosslinking after forming a GO layer. These studies focused on the mechanical properties of the GO materials and showed that the integration improved the connectivity of the GO nanosheets, which led to an increase in both tensile strength and toughness. Covalent crosslinking using dicarboxylic acids by an esterification reaction could control the physical properties of the nanosheet membranes (Figure 2a).[13] The use of various crosslinkers such as ethylene glycol, propandioic acid (PA), glycerol, and neopentyl glycol with different chain lengths can adjust the interlayer spacing and elastic moduli and lead to advances in precise molecular separation. The interlayer spacing of pristine GO is about $0.69\mathsf{n m}$ but is increased to 6-7 nm in an aqueous state due to its high swelling tendency.[14] Covalent crosslinking by hydroxyl groups containing crosslinkers formed a regular interspacing of $0.76\:\mathrm{nm}$ , with less swelling tendency, depending on the type of crosslinker. The crosslinking of $60$ nanosheets also increased the elastic moduli from 0.03 to 3.49 GPa.[13]
Amidation is another method for chemically crosslinking GO that is also widely used for PA preparation.[15] Nam et al. introduced a polycarbonate (PC) support functionalized with branched polyethylenimine (BPEI) and GO nanosheets for a crosslinking reaction (Figure 2b).[15a] After coating BPEI on a porous PC support by immersion in a commercial BPEI solution, a GO solution was filtered to form a GO layer, and carboxyl groups on the GO were crosslinked with amine groups of BPEI. This method avoided excessive intercalation that retained the same interlayer spacing of GO, but greatly improved the dimensional stability over a broad pH range. The resulting composite membranes strongly attached to the polymer support and bonded with other GO nanosheets, improving stability in both acidic (pH 2) and basic (pH 12) aqueous solutions. However, the GO composite membrane still detached in the acidic solution when sonicated because the amide bonds in the crosslinking region were partially converted into amine and carboxylic acid groups. The conversion was attributed to hydrolysis in the presence of protonated ions and the weakened π-π interactions between GO nanosheets in acidic conditions.
Borate crosslinking is one of bio-inspired methods taken from higher-order plants in which borate reinforces the intercellular structure via crosslinking of polysaccharide rhamnogalacturonan II to build stiffened and strong cell walls.[11, 16] This method is applied by co-filtration or separated filtration of an aqueous dispersion of GO and sodium tetraborate followed by low to medium temperature annealing. Borate crosslinking also forms covalent bonds with oxygen-containing hydroxyl groups in GO and increases film stiffness and strength nearly $80\%$ over an untreated film. The use of different types of boronic acids as a crosslinker changed the interlayer spacing from 0.75 to $1.05\:\mathsf{n m}$ , which led to improved hydrogen $(\mathsf{H}_{2})$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacity. Song et al. also used borate crosslinking, inspired by natural nacre, to create super-tough (toughness of $15.3\ \mathsf{M J}/\mathsf{m}^{3})$ artificial nacre that consisted of stacked GO nanosheets with π-π interaction and hydrogel bonding.[17] A similar approach to form a GO composite membrane on a tubular polymer membrane surface was made with a vacuum-assisted preparation method (Figure 2c).[18] Nanochannels were engineered by manipulating the annealing process and the chemistry of boron bridges that affected the molecular transport properties through the membrane. These studies demonstrated the use of GOs as building blocks for nanoporous materials.
Chemical interaction between functional groups of GO and divalent metal ions enhances the mechanical properties of GO nanosheets.[19] While a GO dispersion remains stable in the presence of monovalent metal salts such as LiCl and NaCl, the addition of divalent alkaline salts of $\mathsf{M g C l}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ leads to the agglomeration of GO nanosheets by crosslinking. Park et al. employed this crosslinking method with unmodified GO paper made by filtration on an anodisc membrane filter. Aqueous $\mathsf{M g C l}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ was added to the GO paper to activate the crosslinking reaction (Figure 2d).[19a] This modified GO nanosheet had more tightly bound bridging edges between GO nanosheets ( $\mathsf{x}$ -direction crosslinking) and less tightly bound intercalation between basal planes (y-direction crosslinking). The small amount of divalent metal ions (less than $1w t\%$ ) enhanced the modulus and tensile strength of the GO paper by a simple chemical modification and demonstrated graphene-based materials for macroscale structures. Mono- and divalent metal cations (K+, $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ , $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , Li+, $\mathsf{M g}^{2+},$ ) are capable of fixing the interlayer spacing of GO nanosheets ranged in 1.14 to $1.36\mathsf{n m}$ , but the mixture of salt, i.e. ${\mathsf{K C l}}+{\mathsf{M}}$ $(M=N a C l$ , $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ , LiCl, or ${\mathsf{M g C l}}_{2}.$ ) could reduce the interlayer spacing down to $1.12\ \mathsf{n m}$ in high precision of $0.1\ \mathsf{n m}$ that remained stable over $140~\mathsf{h}.\mathsf{I}^{\mathrm{196}}$ This suggested that GO membranes with the precisely tuned interlayer spacing could present precise molecular separation. Yeh et al. also found a similar result by crosslinking by divalent or trivalent metal ions and showed that GO nanosheets prepared on a porous anodized aluminium oxide (AAO) disc had better stability in water than GO nanosheets prepared on Teflon film due to the existence of $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions.[19c] AAO filter discs, which are very common supports for membrane preparation, especially for 2D materials, are likely to corrode and release ${\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ ions during filtration of an acidic GO dispersion. Other divalent ions such as $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ ions, which are one of by-products during GO synthesis by chemical methods, can also crosslink GO nanosheets and improve the mechanical properties of GO membranes. It was confirmed by additional experiments that modified GO nanosheets by other divalent ions, such as $Z n^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , have increased intrinsic mechanical properties due to the formation of water-stable and less-swollen GO membranes.
Investigation of membrane fabrication methods for scalable process is an essential push of these outstanding membrane materials into industrial levels. Ultrathin GO membranes on supporting layer were conventionally prepared by vacuumassisted filtration or spin-coating methods, advantageous in labscale membrane fabrication. However, some approaches on fabricating graphene-based membranes have been reported using casting or spray-coating method, appropriate for membrane manufacturing (Figure 3). Akbari et al. demonstrated highly ordered thin films of multi-layered GO by shear-alignment approach.[20] Viscoelastic GO ink, that was concentrated from GO aqueous dispersion by adding hydrogel beads, was cast on polymer substrate that nematic phase of GO was aligned forming a highly ordered layer. This method formed GO layer in thickness of $150\ \mathsf{n m}$ for nanofiltration application, but can be modified by reducing layer thickness. Morelos-Gomez et al. reported hybrid layer of GO and few-layered graphene (FLG) with deoxycholate (DOC) by spray-coating of aqueous dispersion.[21] GO/FLG layer in thickness of 26-33 nm was formed on poly(vinyl alcohol)  (PVA) coated polysulfone substrate, followed by thermal treatment at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ crosslinking with $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ solution, enduring high pressure water treatment operation at 50 bar. Spray-coating deposition method is scalable and environmentally friendly that can be applied for other 2D membranes.
GO and polymer nanocomposites also involve the interaction between functional groups on GO and the polymer. Network polymers, such as hydrogels, have been used to combine GO nanosheets in the polymer phase. Two main strategies have been developed for GO and hydrogel nanocomposite: in situ polymerization of water-soluble monomers in GO dispersion, and simple mixing of GO and hydrogel in solution.[22] Various hydrogels such as poly(acrylic acid) (PAA), PVA, polyacrylamide, and poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM) with a crosslinker,  N,N’-methylenebis(acrylamide) (MBA), have been introduced to prepare GO and polymer nanocomposites. Kim et al. incorporated GO with poly(NIPAM-MBA), which is a highly crosslinked hydrogel (Figure 4).[22c] An aqueous solution containing GO nanosheets and monomers were spin-coated on a porous substrate and then polymerized by free radical polymerization initiated under heat treatment with a catalyst. GO nanosheets were crosslinked with the polymer phase without chemical bonds but only bound by poly(NIPAM-MBA), which prevented swelling of the membrane in water. GO nanosheets were also applied as a scaffold for ultrathin membrane fabrication because of the intrinsic 2D nature of GO. Moreover, GO crosslinking resulted in significant improvements in the dimensional stability of the membrane in that it was not delaminated during a desalination operation. Zhong et al. prepared GO and PAA hydrogel nanocomposites by in situ free radical polymerization with ammonium persulfate catalyst (Figure 5).[22b] Ferric nitrate was also introduced for $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions to influence crosslinking of the GO nanosheets. The free radical polymerization was very rapid and trapped the GO nanosheets in the polymer network. Because PAA hydrogel is an elastic material, the GO and PAA composites showed outstanding toughness and stretchability. In this case, a small amount of GO $(0.5\ \mathsf{w t\%})$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions $(0.5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ were employed into the hydrogel phase, but its mechanical properties were greatly improved, with a tensile strength of $777\mathsf{k P a}$ , elongation at break of $2980\%$ , and work of extension of 11.9 MJ $m^{-3}$ . This also showed the potential for medical uses with a great self-healing property that enabled it to automatically heal damage without an external stimulus. Bai et al. introduced PVA hydrogel as a physical crosslinking agent for a nanocomposite that was formed by the assembly of GO nanosheets and crosslinked PVA.[22a] Hydroxyl groups on both GO and PVA enhanced the interfacial interaction and the mixture immediately formed a gel. The GO composite hydrogel was pHsensitive, formed a gel in acidic conditions, and underwent a gelsol transition in alkaline conditions.
## 2.2. 2D metal organic framework materials
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are intrinsically porous materials consisting of metal ions linked by organic bridging ligands that usually form crystalline compounds. Since its first discovery, more than 20,000 different MOFs have been reported with large surface areas, ranging between 1,000 to $10,000\mathrm{m}^{2}/9$ , and well-ordered pore structures in applications such as catalytic transformations, gas adsorption, and proton-conducting membranes.[24] Because of their highly ordered structure and strong chemical bonds, MOFs are three-dimensional (3D) crystals when prepared without dimensional controls.[25] However, for use in membrane-based separation processes, fabrication methods for thin MOF layers have been investigated because membrane thickness is an important parameter to improve molecular transport properties. For example, the secondary growth method of zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) was introduced to effectively control and orientate ZIF crystals on a substrate and a defect-free thin layer of ZIFs with a controlled thickness was synthesized.[26] Some other approaches to form MOF nanosheets were developed by using 2D ligands as a linker.
Layered MOFs prepared by stacking 2D MOF layers, with relatively weak hydrogen bonding interaction or π-π interaction from planar organic molecules, are known to be easily exfoliated into nanosheets by various methods of mechanical processing, sonication, or chemical exfoliation.[27] Peng et al. introduced a mechanical exfoliation method from layered Zn2(benzimidazole)3 $(Z\mathsf{n}_{2}(\mathsf{b i m})_{3})$ and $Z n_{2}(6i m)_{4}$ precursors by ball milling, resulting in MOF nanosheets with sub-10 nm thickness and $0.29\ \mathsf{n m}$ honeycomb-like pores.[28] Because physical exfoliation might damage the structure of MOFs, ball milling at a very low speed of
60 rpm followed by ultrasonication in a volatile solvent facilitated the formation of single layered $Z n_{2}(6i m)_{4}$ nanosheets.[28b]  After exfoliation of $Z n_{2}(6i m)_{3}$ , the MOF nanosheets were coated on α- ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ disks to form ultrathin composite membranes (Figure 6).[28a] The volatile solvent, such as methanol or propanol, penetrated between the layers of the layered MOFs and stabilized the exfoliated nanosheets. The aperture size of a four-membered ring, ${\sim}0.21~\mathsf{n m}$ , helped separate small gas molecules for membranebased separation applications. Unlike exfoliation methods, the surfactant assisted method is also a facile synthesis method for ultrathin layered and nonlayered MOF nanosheets in high yield that can be applied to most MOF structures reported to date.[29] Cao et al. developed a method that used polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as a surfactant molecule that attached onto the MOFs at the early stage of synthesis and allowed the vertical growth of MOF crystals, called a 2D porphyrin paddlewheel framework-3 (PPF-3).[29a] The thickness of this MOF nanosheet could be tuned by controlling the concentration of the starting materials such that the thickness of the ultrathin PPF-3 nanosheets was $11.9\pm4.2$ nm. These methods developed for 2D MOFs can be applied to various structures and properties of MOF nanosheets. Pustovarenko et al. demonstrated the preparation of freestanding lamellae of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al), CAU-10(Al), and $N H_{2}$ -CAU-10(Al).[29c] The use of surfactant induced crystal growth of MOFs to xydirection fabricated ultrathin nanosheets of MOFs with uniformed 3D pore structures (Figure 7). This approach would lead to the efficient utilization of any MOFs into ultrathin nanosheets for membrane application.
## 2.3. Other 2D materials
Although graphene-based materials and MOFs are of great interest, other 2D materials such as zeolites, molybdenum disulphide, and COFs have shown their potential as 2D materials. Hexagonal layer of boron nitride (BN) or graphite carbon nitride $(g-C_{3}N_{4})$ are considered as metal-free 2D materials. Other hexagonal layer of metal atoms (M) sandwiched with layers of chalcogen atoms (X) in a ${\sf M X}_{2}$ structure, known as transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) are also a promising 2D nanosheets including ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{W S}}_{2}$ . Transition metal carbides or nitrides, known as MXenes with a formula of $\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{n}-1}\mathsf{X}_{\mathsf{n}}\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{x}}$ (M=transition metal, $x=c$ and/or N, ${\sf T}_{\mathrm{x}=:}$ surface functional group such as F, OH, or O) are also a candidate as membrane materials. Zeolites are synthetic or natural materials with microporous and crystalline properties that are usually used for catalysis, adsorption, and ion exchange applications.[30] The layered intermediates of zeolites, called 2D zeolites, have been developed based on the discovery of a layered precursor available for possible post-synthetic manipulations. Unlike MOFs, COFs are framework materials linked by strong covalent bonds without metal-organic bonds. The functional diversity of the organic linkers expands the possible molecular structures and the range of physical properties.[31] COFs are intrinsically 2D or 3D crystals, depending on their building blocks, and the covalent bond of 2D COFs are restricted to the 2D plane and stacked to form a layered structure. Due to the difficulty in controlling the reaction to crystallize linked organic polymers, methods for reversible formation of covalent bonds are widely used, such as solvothermal synthesis and microwave synthesis.[31] The synthesized 2D COFs are typically exfoliated nanosheets from layered COFs.  Li et al. prepared 2D COF-1 membranes, which were synthesised from 1,4-benzenediboronic acid in a 1,4-dioxane/mesitylene mixture, by ultrafiltration exfoliation followed by assembly on a porous substrate.[32] The 2D COF-1 membranes exhibited decreased surface area, pore volume, and average pore size because of the loss of the original regular channels caused by the random stacking of the nanosheets. However, the nanosheets that were coated in an ultrathin film (thickness of $100\ n m)$ ) had improved molecular transport properties. Peng et al. prepared COF nanosheets from synthetic monomers, called 1,3,5-tris(4-formylphenyl)benzene and commercial monomers of tris(4-aminophenyl)amine and tris(4-formylphenyl)amine that had $1.8~\mathsf{n m}$ pores and $0.39\:\mathsf{n m}$ of interlayer distance when stacked (Figure 8).[33] The COFs with highly ordered hexagonal networks and sheet-like morphology were easily exfoliated by ultrasonication due to the weak interlayer stacking force of the flexible building block. A facile synthetic protocol of COFs or porous organic frameworks (POFs) is capable when polymer synthesis technique such as interfacial polymerisation (IP) is applied.[34] Shan et al. developed ultrathin POF membranes, called benzimidazole-linked polymers (BILP101x) using IP.[34a] The  IP method has been applied in large scale for preparation of polyamide membranes that two different monomers dissolved in different immiscible solvents are reacted at the interface, fabricating ultrathin membranes. A defect-free layer of BILP-101x with a thickness of $400\ \mathsf{n m}$ was formed on porous substrate from 1,2,4,5-benzenetetramine tetrahydrochloride (BTA) aqueous solution and 1,3,5- triformylbenzene (TFB) benzene solution by a few second reaction. The use of appropriate monomers enabled the fabrication of microporous and ultrathin membranes. JimenezSolomon et al. introduced rigid and contorted structures to synthesise highly crosslinked polyarylate nanofilms down to 20 nm in thickness that demonstrated outstanding performance in molecular separation.[34b] Matsumoto et al. reported that iminelinked 2D COFs can be fabricated by IP method with a use of catalyst.[34c] The membrane thickness was controlled in a range of $2.5\mathsf{n m}$ to $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ that amorphous thin film became crystalline thick film. These methods can be extended to membranes with tunable properties due to the rich chemistry, commercially available and up-scaling feasible.
BNs are hexagonal planes constructed by B and N atoms alternatively linked together via strong B-N covalent bonds. BN is a planar material like graphene, and the crystalline surface of BN brings valuable properties such as high thermal conductivity and mechanical strength. Unlike carbon materials, BN has strong luminescence in the ultraviolet range that makes BN attractive for blue light and ultraviolet emission.[35] BN nanosheets are typically prepared from hexagonal BN micro-sized particles by ultrafiltration with a strong organic solvent.[36] More recently, a preparation method for BN nanosheets by ball milling was developed that was scalable for mass production, low cost, and high yield.[35] During exfoliation, BN nanosheets were functionalised with amino and hydroxyl groups that increased BN’s affinity with other organic molecules. The prepared BN nanosheets consisted of a few layers with a total thickness of 2.5 nm and dimensions below $100\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 9a). Functionalised BN nanosheets were introduced into the polymer phase as a filler to improve molecular permeation and separation properties of the polymer membranes without loss of mechanical flexibility of the polymer (Figure 9b).[35b] Chen et al. also prepared highly watersoluble, functionalized BN by ball milling with urea as a functional agent, and their membrane for solvent separation was fabricated on a porous substrate by vacuum-assisted filtration.[37] $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ has been attracted owing to their tunable properties that can be adopted to various applications such as photocatalysis.[7a] $g-C_{3}N_{4}$ can be usually synthesised by thermal polymerisation of precursors and their nanosheets are prepared by chemical exfoliation with strong acid, high temperature calcination under ${\sf H}_{2}$ or sonication-assisted liquid exfoliation depending on properties of raw $g-C_{3}N_{4}$ .[38] Unlike BN, small pores of $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ with geometric diameter of 0.31 to $0.34\:\mathsf{n m}$ provide extra pathway for transport of small molecules with nanochannels as 2D membranes.[38c]
TMDs, such as ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{M o S e}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{W T e}_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{W S}_{2}$ were developed as graphene-like 2D materials and widely studied due to their unique electrical, optical, thermal and mechanical properties with various structures and synthetic routes.[39]  Among them, molybdenum disulfide $(\mathsf{M o S}_{2})$ and tungsten disulfide $(\mathsf{W S}_{2})$ are representative 2D materials synthesised by vapor phase deposition such as sulfurisation, thermal deposition, or vapor phase reaction.[39a] TMD nanosheets are impermeable materials, but provide molecular transport property through nanochannels, antibacterial property for water filtration, or high adsorption property for certain gases.[40] $\mathsf{T i}_{3}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{T}_{\times}$ has been a widely explored MXene that their hydrophilic surface in negative charge provides similar properties to GO.[41] $\mathsf{T i}_{3}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{T}_{\times}$ nanosheets in multilayer are prepared from ${\bar{\mathsf{T i}}}_{3}{\mathsf{A l C}}_{3}$ powder by selective etching of Al layer in acid solution, then single or few-layer flakes are prepared by delamination in aqueous state with ultrasonication. Representative structures of other 2D materials are described in Figure 10. The preparation methods of TMDs, $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ , or BNs are rather simpler than GO with the possibility of large-scale synthesis.
