## 3. Applications
Nanosheet membranes have demonstrated their extraordinary properties in molecular transport, but are still in the research stage because they require more investigation for largescale applications. Membrane processes in ion separation, gas separation, and water processing are well-established industrial processes in which nanosheet membranes will play a great role when large-scale membrane fabrication and modulation methods are achieved. Graphene-based membranes are the pioneers of 2D nanosheet membranes that already have shown potential in large-scale membrane fabrication for ion separation and gas separation. The first report of GO and rGO membranes from Nair et al. demonstrated impermeable gas transport of small molecules, but unhindered water vapor permeation through the membranes showed the possibility of graphene-based materials for membrane separation applications.[42] Ultrathin GO membranes form nanochannels between nanosheets that provide pathways for molecular transport (Figure 11a).[43] However, their dimensional instability in aqueous or humidified environments limits their practical application. Various methods and materials have been introduced to improve their stability and some of the methods are under investigation for practical applications. Other advanced materials such as MOFs and COFs provide unique molecular transport properties through their highly ordered pores. These membranes have been investigated to minimize membrane thickness with improved physical properties for practical applications, and the fabrication methods of their nanosheets have attracted attention to maximize process productivity in membrane processes. Ultrathin MOF or COF membranes form nanochannels between nanosheets as well as nanopores on nanosheets that provide highly selective molecular transport (Figure 11b),[28a] presenting high productivity and purity of membrane processes.
## 3.1. Gas separation
Molecular transport through nanosheet membranes was first theoretically investigated by quantum mechanical calculations that showed that small pores generated on graphene or GO can enable selective gas transport for certain gases.[44] Stacked graphene or GO also form nanochannels between nanosheets, called the interlayer spacing, and the width of the nanochannels is determined by functional groups of graphene or GO that influence gas permeation and separation properties. Kim et al. demonstrated selective gas transport of small molecules through a few, layered GO membranes with thickness $<10\mathsf{n m}$ .[45] The membranes had the ultrafast gas transport property of ${\sf H}_{2}$ and selectivity over other gases. Interestingly, the gas transport properties varied depending on the experimental conditions such as temperature and humidity. GO membranes are likely to swell in high-humidity environments that increase the interlayer spacing of GO nanosheets, but water molecules accumulated in nanochannels hinder gas transport, except for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ transport. Usually gas transport properties of conventional polymer membranes are changed by temperature and humidity mainly due to changes in gas activation behaviour,[46] but for GO membranes, the gas transport properties change because the nanochannel structure becomes unstable.
One approach to improve the mechanical properties of a membrane and form an ultrathin layer is to crosslink the GO nanosheets with borate. Wang et al. prepared a GO-borate membrane with $8\mathsf{n m}$ thickness by vacuum-assisted filtration and thermal treatment.[47] Depending on the thermal treatment temperature, the interlayer spacing was controlled between 0.68 to $0.79\mathsf{n m}$ , the change of interlayer spacing between wet and dry states was small, and the membrane did not lose separation performance in a humidified state with an industrial gas stream. The gas permeation properties of the membrane had a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance of 650 GPU ( $1~{\mathsf{G P U}}=10^{-6}~{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}({\mathsf{S T P}})~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-2}~{\mathsf{s}}^{-1}~{\mathsf{c m H}}~{\mathsf{g}}$ -1 $=3.3\times10^{-10}\mathsf{m o l}\mathsf{m}^{-2}\mathsf{s}^{-1}\mathsf{P}\mathsf{a}^{-1})$ and selectivity of 75 over methane $({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4})$ , which exceeded other GO membranes and was even comparable to zeolites or ultrathin polymers. Yan et al. introduced a small amount of GO to a chitosan membrane and followed the crosslinking with borate with a thermal treatment to improve its mechanical and barrier properties such that only $1.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ each of boron and GO in chitosan increased the tensile strength by $160\%$ and surpassed the original oxygen permeability by $10\%$ .[48] This was mainly because the GO nanosheets and crosslinked network increased the tortuosity of the pathway for gas transport and decreased the gas diffusion. Yang et al. prepared thioureacrosslinked GO (TU-GO) membranes through  a  facile hydrothermal  self-assembly  synthesis by immersing a ceramic support tube in a GO and thiourea solution at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an alkaline condition $\left({\mathsf{p}}{\mathsf{H}}\ =\ 8\right)$ that caused covalent crosslinking.[49] The thickness of the TU-GO layer was controlled from $70\ \mathsf{n m}$ to several microns by using a controlled reaction time and solution concentration. The membrane presented a unique ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance of over $68\times10^{-8}$ mol $\mathfrak{m}^{\cdot2}\ \mathfrak{s}^{\cdot1}\ \mathsf{P}\mathsf{a}^{\cdot1}$ (2,060 GPU) and an ideal selectivity of over 150 for any other gas molecules. The membrane also had membrane performance for dehydration via a pervaporation process that demonstrated nearly complete rejection of alcohol and ions while permeating water vapor.
GO nanosheets are used as a building block or a scaffold of ultrathin membranes, although they are often used to suppress gas transport and improve barrier properties. Because membrane thickness is important to increase gas permeance, membrane fabrication with an ultrathin membrane layer has been investigated, but has often led to defects on the membrane that eliminated its separation properties. Kim et al. introduced GO nanosheets into polybenzoxazole-co-imide, called a thermally rearranged polymer, with a thickness of $35~\mathsf{n m}$ .[50] A polymer solution with GO nanosheets was spin-coated on an AAO disc as a substrate and GO nanosheets uniformly coated the substrate without defects. The membrane demonstrated an improved $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance of 1,784 GPU while maintaining a gas selectivity that was 482 times higher than the freestanding membrane film.
MOFs have widely been investigated as gas separation membranes due to their unique crystalline and pore structures. 2D MOFs provide well-ordered nanopores as well as nanochannels that are advantageous for membrane applications, compared to graphene-based nanosheets. MOF membranes usually have been introduced as a thin film composite on a substrate or a MOF-polymer composite, called a mixed matrix membrane (MMM), but micron-sized 3D crystals often agglomerated and restricted the integration of MOFs in the polymer phase. 2D nanosheet fillers of MOFs are advantageous in incorporating fillers into polymers or building ultrathin 2D membranes for membrane processes. Rodenas et al. introduced 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (CuBDC) MOF to synthesise MOF nanosheets from bulk CuBDC MOF by the diffusion-mediated modulation of the MOF growth kinetics.[51] This MOF crystal morphology evolved based on the synthetic temperature from ultrathin nanosheets $(<10\mathsf{n m})$ at a low temperature of $298\mathsf{K}$ to thicker platelets at a high temperature of $333\ \mathsf{K}.$ The same methodology can be adapted to other layered MOFs by using alternative metal nodes such as ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $Z n^{2+}$ to tune the porosity and functionality of MOFs. These 2D MOF nanosheets were incorporated into a commercial $\mathsf{P l}$ called Matrimid® 5218 to fabricate MMMs and MOF nanosheets that were uniformly distributed through the entire membrane, whereas the regular MOF crystals usually formed fractures or defects between crystals and the polymer phases. Pustovarenko et al. prepared nonlayered MOF nanosheets through a surfactant-assisted method and fabricated MMMs with Matrimid® 5218.[29c] This membrane presented an improvement of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability from pristine Matrimid® 5218 by almost $20\%$ as well as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ selectivity after adding MOF nanosheets in $16\mathrm{\Omega}\mathrm{wt}\%$ . Peng et al. prepared poly $\scriptstyle(Z\cap_{2}({\mathsf{b e n z i m i d a z o l e}})_{4})$ $(Z\mathsf{n}_{2}(\mathsf{b i m})_{4})$ nanosheet membranes coated on $a-A l_{2}O_{3}$ discs as molecular sieve nanosheets.[28b] $Z n_{2}(\mathsf{b i m})_{4}$ nanosheets were prepared from bulk $Z n_{2}(\mathsf{b i m})_{4}$ by ball milling and were coated by vacuum filtration. The resulting membrane demonstrated ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance of up to 2,700 GPU with ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C O}_{2}$ selectivity of 291. Wang et al. introduced $N i_{8}(5-$ bbdc) $6(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O H})_{4})$ structure, called Mesh Adjustable Molecular Sieve (MAMS-1) due to their excellent hydrothermal stability and 2D layered structure with interplanar distances of 2.9 and $5.0{\mathrm{~\AA}}.$ [52] This MAMS-1 membranes presented a sharp cut-off of the permeance between He and ${\sf H}_{2}$ gases and larger ones. Hu et al. incorporated GO as a seeding layer during ZIF-8 synthesis and formed ZIF-8/GO nanosheet membranes on AAO as well as on Nylon substrates.[53] Incorporating GO into ZIF-8 greatly reduced the membrane thickness to $\mathord{\sim}100$ nm and improved gas permeance. The membrane had an ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance of over $5.46\times$ $10^{-8}$ mol $\mathsf{m}^{-2}\mathsf{s}^{-1}\mathsf{P}\mathsf{a}^{-1}$ (165 GPU). Similarly, $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ was also used as a template layer of ZIF-8, exhibiting ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance of 203 GPU.[54] Shan et al. prepared ultrathin layer of BILP-101x on γ- $A l_{2}O_{3}$ support by IP that with high temperature stability that exhibited ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance of up to 24.3 GPU with ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C}{\sf O}_{2}$ selectivity of 39.7.[34a]
Other 2D materials, such as COF, MXene, ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{W S}_{2}$ , BN or $g{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ also presented performance in gas separation application. Ultrathin ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ membranes prepared by Wang et al. showed very high ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance up to 27440 GPU based on Knudsen diffusion.[55] Freestanding 2D MXene membranes with thickness of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , prepared by Ding et al. presented excellent ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeability and selectivity over other gases $({\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ selectivity of 160) with stable long-term performance over 700 h of continuous operation.[41c] Another MXene membrane fabricated on AAO substrate by vacuum filtration by Shen et al., presented defect-free layer with controllable thickness of 5 to $80\mathsf{n m}$ .[56] This MXene membrane demonstrated precise ${\sf H}_{2}$ transport with ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance of 1584 GPU and ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C O}_{2}$ selectivity of 27. However, after crosslinking with borate and polyethylenimine and following $75\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ treatment (MBP-75), $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeance was remarkably enhanced. Incorporation of nanosheets such as $\mathsf{W S}_{2}$ or BN into polymer phase to form MMMs gas permeability and/or selectivity. $10\%$ introduction of $\mathsf{W S}_{2}$ into fluorinated poly(phenylene oxide)
(FPPO) improved $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ permeability, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{4}$ selectivity by $216\%$ , $148\%$ , and $137\%$ , respectively, because $\mathsf{W S}_{2}$ provided transport channels for gas molecules.[40c] Similarly, introduction of functionalised BN nanosheets into thermally rearranged polymers  by 1 $w t\%$ increased $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity from 24.1 to 275.[35b]
Table 1 summarizes the gas transport properties of 2D nanocomposite membranes based on GO and MOFs. Not many membranes with remarkable performance have been reported, but the investigation of 2D nanocomposite membranes is growing. When nanosheets were introduced as scaffolds for membrane fabrication, their gas transport properties were mostly affected by raw materials properties. In this case, gas selectivity was maintained, but gas permeance was significantly increased because of the reduced membrane thickness of the nanosheets. MOF-based 2D membranes exhibited greater ${\sf H}_{2}$ permeance, which came from the intrinsic properties of MOFs because MOF membranes usually provide unique nanopores suitable for ${\sf H}_{2}$ molecular transport.
## 3.2. Ion separation
Ion separation is a widely applied process in water treatment and in the food, chemical, and pharmaceutical industries.[1] Nanosheet membranes were first used in ion separation applications for water or liquid treatment processes such as nanofiltration and ultrafiltration.[57] Although Joshi et al. reported that the GO laminates presented a sharp molecular cut-off at 0.45 nm for ion and molecular sieving,[58] their natural tendency to swell in the aqueous state increased their instability in water.[14] Preventing their swelling tendency during the separation process and keeping the high membrane performance are the key issues for graphene-based membranes.
Crosslinking of graphene or GO nanosheets is the most commonly considered method to prevent swelling of nanosheets because chemical bonds created on the crosslinking sites to limit the dispersion of the nanosheets in water. Jia et al. introduced covalent crosslinking of GO membranes with dicarboxylic acid.[13] After forming a GO layer on a substrate, dicarboxylic acid crosslinking agents with different chain lengths and structures were introduced that varied the interlayer distance of the GO nanosheets. Depending on the crosslinking agents used, the membrane mechanical properties and permeation flux for various ions were changed. For a pristine GO membrane, the ion permeation flux of KCl and $\mathsf{K}_{4}[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N}_{6})]$ were similar, but after crosslinking, KCl the flux showed a minor increase, but the $\mathsf{K}_{4}[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N}_{6})]$ flux decreased. However, when GO nanosheets were crosslinked with a long linear crosslinker, octanedioic acid, the membrane demonstrated a major flux increase in both KCl and ${\sf K}_{4}[{\sf F e}({\sf C}{\sf N}_{6})]$ . Controlled crosslinking by the use of the salt mixture, i.e. ${\mathsf{K C l}}+{\mathsf{M}}$ $_{M=N a C l}$ , $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ , LiCl, or $\mathsf{M g C l}_{2}$ ) achieved ion sieving through GO membrane.[19b] KCl treated GO membrane with a thickness of $750~\mathsf{n m}$ showed low permeation rate of $\mathsf{N a^{+}}$ , $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ below detection rate by the single ion permeate test. The ion flux changes were mainly due to size-selectivity of hydrate ions from the different interlayer spacings. Lim et al. introduced GO membranes crosslinked to polyethyleneimine (PEI) with tannic acid (TA) in an alkaline condition $\mathsf{(p H=8.5)}$ ) in which TA reacts well with GO by oxidative coupling polymerization and with the amine groups on PEI, improving the dimensional stability.[59] The crosslinked membrane exhibited a high water flux of $15.4L\mathsf{m}^{-2}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ with NaCl and $\mathsf{M g S O_{4}}$ rejection of $66\%$ and $82\%$ , respectively. This GO-based membrane also showed a great antibacterial property, mainly due to the TA moiety.
Abraham et al. reported the GO membrane exhibited tunable sieving of ions.[60] Physically confined GO nanosheets in a sealed container were naturally swelled in different humidity conditions to control their interlayer spacing and were immediately glued. The interlayer spacing of GO nanosheets increased linearly as the relative humidity during preparation was controllably increased. The ion permeation rates exponentially decreased by decreasing the channel size, but the effects on water transport were weak. The graphene-based membranes exhibited $97\%$ rejection for $_{\mathsf{N a C l}}$ , which showed a possible route for desalination applications. Akbari et al. prepared GO nanofiltration membranes in large-area by casting concentrated GO ink that exhibited retention of salts $(\mathsf{M g C l}_{2}$ , $N a_{2}S O_{4}$ , NaCl, and $\mathsf{M g S O_{4}},$ ) around 30 to $37\%$ . In this case, modification might improve salt rejection property.[20]
MOF membranes with a narrow pore size distribution are also of great interest for ion separation applications. Well defined pore structures can control ion transport properties such as selective ion transport. Zhang et al. prepared an ultrathin ZIF-8 membrane by a GO-assisted interfacial growth on AAO substrate that quickly and selectively transports ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{i}}^{+}$ over other alkali metal ions.[61] ZIF-8/GO layer was formed with a thickness of $\sim400\ \mathsf{n m}$ with $\mathord{\sim}200$ -nm-sized ZIF-8 crystals were observed. Ion transport properties were mainly based on the pore size of the MOFs and the ion diameters. The ion selectivity of the $Z|F-8/G0$ membranes for $\mathsf{L i^{+}/R b^{+}}$ , $\mathsf{L i^{+}}/\mathsf{K^{+}}$ , and ${\mathsf{L i}}^{+}/{\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ were 4.6, 2.2, and 1.4, respectively, which was the highest ever reported for synthetic membranes due to the small difference in ionic diameters.
Unlike GO membranes, ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ membranes demonstrated excellent anti-swelling property in a stable interlayer spacing of $1.2\mathsf{n m}$ without crosslinking of nanosheets that was advantageous in synthetic procedure.[39b] Presenting high water flux of $140\mathsf{L}\mathsf{m}^{\cdot}$ $^{2}{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ at 0.7 bar from $500\mathsf{n m}$ thick ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ membrane, but separation performance for ions were around 55 to $70\%$ . $g-C_{3}N_{4}$ membranes coated on AAO support also demonstrated high water flux in nanofiltration range from their $160\mathsf{n m}$ thick layer, but showed no ion rejection performance.[38e]
## 3.3. Water desalination
Membrane-based water desalination is one of the most widely applied processes to produce potable water from saline water. It commonly consists of hydraulic pressure applied for an RO process and osmotic pressure applied for a forward osmosis (FO) process. However, not many materials have been chosen for this process due to limitations in performance, and some polymer membranes such as CA and PA in thin film composites dominate the membrane desalination market.[62] Despite the great molecular transport of water molecules through GO membranes, because of dimensional stability they have a low rejection of small ions such as NaCl, which is a major component to be removed from saline water.[14] Numerous graphene-based membranes have been investigated to improve salt rejection properties for water desalination processes, but not many membranes exhibited sufficiently high salt rejection to be suitable for practical desalination applications.
FO processes do not require excessive mechanical properties because the process does not apply high pressure on the membranes to drive water transport. Therefore, ultrathin freestanding membranes can be used for the FO process. Liu et al. prepared freestanding, ultrathin reduced GO membranes that exhibited high water flux and salt rejection in an FO process with minimized internal concentration polarization issues.[63] The GO solution was first filtered on a cellulose nitrate membrane filter by the vacuum filtration method, then delaminated under hydriodic acid vapor. The GO was reduced during delamination by acid vapor exposure and a freestanding GO membrane was obtained. The membrane thickness was controlled from $20~\mathsf{n m}$ to $500~\mathsf{n m}$ and the membrane with $100~\mathsf{n m}$ thickness demonstrated a water flux of around $25\mathsf{L}\mathsf{m}^{-2}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ . Moreover, rGO membranes presented much lower ion permeability than GO membranes or commercial cellulose triacetate membranes. Chemical crosslinking of GO with PA is the way to develop desalination membranes because PA is widely used for membrane desalination processes. Jin et al. introduced crosslinking of GO with diamine and trimethyl chloride monomers by using amide-bonding between the acid groups on the GO and the amide groups of the crosslinker, and intercrosslinking between parallel GO nanosheets was achieved by forming a PA structure.[64]
Physical crosslinking of GO nanosheets by entwining them with a network polymer is one way to prevent swelling of GO membranes. Kim et al. introduced a network polymer, called poly(NIPAM-MBA) into a GO nanosheet during membrane preparation for use in RO and FO applications. This crosslinking method suppressed swelling of GO nanosheets in water and improved their salt rejection capability while maintaining their water flux. The membrane prepared on a Nylon substrate by spincoating ,with a thickness of less than $40\ \mathsf{n m}$ , demonstrated a water flux of $25.8\textsf{L m}^{-2}\textsf{h}^{-1}$ , with $_{\mathsf{N a C l}}$ rejection of $99.9\%$ in FO process,[22c] and the membrane fabricated by casting and filtration method exhibited water flux of $33.5\mathsf{L}\mathsf{m}^{-2}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ with $N a C l$ rejection of $98.5\%$ at 10 bar in an RO process.[23] This membrane also showed great chlorine tolerance that significantly simplified the desalination process and extend membrane lifetime because oxidation agents, such as chlorine, that are widely used for disinfection during water processing, are likely to degrade membrane structures. The preparation method based on casting and vacuum filtration showed the potential for large-scale membrane fabrication because continuous casting is the way to produce flat sheet membranes at an industrial level. Crosslinking of GO/FLG layers by thermal treatment and ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ crosslinking with DOC greatly improved NaCl rejection of the membrane to $85\%$ and mechanical resistance to 50 bar.[21] The NaCl rejection mechanism was due to Gibbs–Donnan exclusion by surface functional groups of GO, DOC and the zwitterionic behaviour by the combination of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ and DOC.
## 4. Summary and outlook
The recent development of 2D materials and their composite membranes have opened the next generation in membrane processes for gas, ion, and liquid separations, which have presented better performance based on the unique properties of membranes. After development of chemical or physical exfoliation, or in-situ fabrication methods to prepare 2D materials, their membrane applications have been investigated resulting in the formation of ultrathin layers with well-ordered nanopores and/or nanochannels. GO-based membranes as a pioneer of 2D materials membrane have presented remarkable performance in gas separation and possible liquid separation by membrane modification. Crosslinking of GO membranes by chemical or physical methods improved dimensional stability of GO membranes in water, qualifying them for water desalination applications with long-term performance stability. Development in fabrication methods of graphene-based membranes from modified GO solution demonstrated potential for large-scale manufacturing by conventional methods such as casting, spincoating, and spray-coating that have been applied for polymerbased membranes. Crosslinking of GO also enables tuning the sizes of nanochannels that effect the molecular transport properties, improving rejection of monovalent and divalent ions. Other 2D materials such as MOFs, COFs, BNs, $\mathsf{g-C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4},$ , TMDs, and MXene were fabricated into ultrathin composite membranes by solution filtration or interfacial polymerisation, or mixed matrix membranes with organic polymers. They demonstrated great molecular transport properties. Their intrinsic stability based on their firm crystalline structure has improved the stability of the membranes. Functionalization of 2D materials also improved affinity with polymers when they were mixed to form a polymer nanocomposite. The inherent nano-sized pores of MOFs and COFs are also advantageous for molecular transport through the nanopores. It is anticipated that the development of 2D MOFs or COFs will bring a breakthrough for membrane applications.
Despite a great progress has been achieved, several emerging challenges are remained for industrial applications of 2D based membranes. First, scalable preparation methods for 2D materials in controlled properties should be developed. The latest 2D materials, such as 2D MOFs and BN, require complex synthetic methodology or lengthy reaction time from low concentration solution that leads to much effort in preparation and produces vast volume of liquid waste. Some synthetic methods also require synthetic monomers for 2D material preparations such as COFs. Although graphene-based materials are prepared in aqueous solution in large-scale, regulating the quality of the materials by manufacturing sources should be proceeded. Second, the well-dispersed properties of GO in water led to performance instability of those membranes and poor salt rejection properties in water processing. Intrinsic degradation property of MOFs in water also hinders application in water desalination, therefore breakthrough in materials development is essential, considering the market size of membrane liquid separations. Lastly, issues on membrane fabrication in uniform quality remain. The controlled solution-based methods could prepare an ultrathin membrane layer of 2D materials by filtration coating that are not feasible for continuous membrane fabrication process. Although a study showed GO membrane fabrication by casting of GO ink, modification is required for higher separation performance. To be successfully applied for industrial membrane processes, membrane fabrication over large areas and modulation are essential to deal with the large volume of raw substances to be purified into valuable products by a continuous process. Membrane fabrication in thin film composites or hollow fibres, the industrial standard for membrane formation, would make 2D nanosheet membranes an attractive alternative to current polymer-based membranes, but it requires other strategies for materials research investigations. Proper incorporation of 2D materials to polymers as templates to improve the original performance of polymer membranes will also be a promising and relatively facile method to advance their performance. Various approaches to overcome the current issues on 2D membranes have constantly reported in both academic and industrial aspects that will advance the membrane processes and implement further potential applications in nanofiltration and energy-related applications.
